>
> Similarly, a player would have to be most unlucky to meet an arch-lich 
> in the mines, or a Demogorgon bones file, or a goblin with a wand of 
> death, but these are all YAADs.

Well, YAADs are a "feature" of Nethack.

Whether that's a good or bad thing is certainly up for debate, but
trying to remove them entirely leads us to Final Fantasy, in which all
the tools required to solve a problem are provided to the player _just_
before he encounters the problem itself.  (FF7 and beyond, that is.  The
earlier ones are a lot better in this regard.)

I like Final Fantasy.  But for different reasons than I like Nethack.

> If it is possible for a doubled wizard to wipe out your GDSM in 4 turns, 
> then it *will* eventually happen to someone. And then the touch of death 
> will be next, and the player will be mighty peeved.

Indeed he will.  About as peeved as the guy wielding the Tsurugi who has
half spell damage and MR, and yet both Rodney's available curse attacks
end up targeting the Tsurugi, which fails its resistance check both
times.

> Fixing armour is tough mid-combat; it takes one turn to get confused and 
> one turn to read the scroll at least, not to mention de-bagging the 
> scroll and removing nonbroken armour.

In a circumstance such as the above, I'd be inclined to say "lethal",
not just "tough".

> Having said all this I like the new destroy armour idea (haven't seen it 
> in practice, though).

The parenthetical aside seems to be the perspective of some of the 
more vociferous objectors as well.

With regards to those objectors... I'm certainly willing to address and/or 
discuss any concerns players may have about any of my changes, but I must
admit that I'm always going to be inclined to take concerns generated from 
actual play a bit more seriously than hypotheticals from people who
haven't even tried playing it at all.

